 how can white  actually draw this game and not lose  well that comes down to having multiple  threats from the king and it starts with  the move king to g7 now it's first look  at why this is so important so the most  common play that black could move is  king to b6 they don't have to we'll also  look at h4 as well but let's look at  king 2  b6 now white can play the move  king to f6  if black takes the pawn on c6 well you  can probably see pretty easily white can  play g5 and now they can stop the pawn  from pushing forward okay well if black  doesn't take the pawn on c6 maybe they  say okay that's not going to work i need  to start pushing my pawn here if they  play h4  white has the move king to e5 and this  entire setup  is all anchored on the fact that white  comes to e5 because now we see the  multiple threats that black has to deal  with  the first one is if black takes the pawn  on c6  well y can come to f4  and they can stop the pawn from  promoting down here  if instead  they don't take on c6 what if they come  down and play h3  well in this case white can now come to  d6 and this is the next critical square  that white's coming to and you can see  the threat that white has because they  can push forward with c7 and then they  can play c8  without worrying about black capturing  that pawn so how does black move from  now they could  play a7  and then we could just play d7 and then  push up our pawn or they could say well  i'm going to go ahead and promote to a  queen  so they play h2  and then we play c7  okay well now queen to  our pawn promoting to h1 but now we get  our own queen  so in this position we have been able to  actually  stabilize and this is going to be a  drawn game  now if we come back uh we looked at what  happens if after we come to e5 if they  play h3  we're just going to push forward with  that c7 but what if instead they decide  yeah we're just going to take that  pawn on c6 we already looked at we play  f4 and we're going to capture that pawn  so it all comes back to originally  instead of playing c7 instead of playing  h7 it all comes to the g7 square because  we're threatening coming to f6 and then  e5 so that we have two threats one of  coming to d6 helping the c6 pawn  as well as preparing to come over and  attack the pawn  on the h file black has to figure out  which side they want to deal with  but at the end of the day they are left  with a  draw so subtle moves white's  saying i can get to two different  places using a different path same  number of moves but a different path so  that's the concept we're going to start  looking at this in some actual games  so this is the next variation take a  minute pause and see if you can come up  with black's best move  now the best move for black is to play  king 2 b2 but let's first look at some  of the alternatives and why they won't  work now we have the pawn on a4 we're  trying to come down to a one in white on  f2  they can first move with pawn you can  always move two squares they're coming  to f4 and then they're going to push up  to f8  if we try to immediately come to a3  well that's not going to work because  then we they just capture our pawn and  then we start trying to catch up to  this f pawn  and we never can so that doesn't work  if we try to immediately chase this pawn  on f2 we play c2  they play a four  we try to catch it with d3 but they push  forward you can see we're never going to  catch that pawn  so let's say we change course and play  a3 okay well they're just going to  capture the pawn that's not going  to work  so  if we try to commit to either side  neither of those are the best option  the best option is to have a dual threat  and that happens to be king to b2 now we  have to look at why is that the best  option well now white has to decide  do they want to  take the pawn or do they want to push  forward if they take the pawn well now  we can actually catch this pawn on the  f-file  they play f4 we play d4 and you can see  we are going to capture that pawn  because we made them commit we had a  dual threat with our king if they don't  take the pawn  if they start pushing with f4  well not only is it not a draw  and not only are we not going to lose  but we are going to win this game  because now we have the dual threat of  a3 they can't take our pawn anymore  that's why king to b2 is so powerful  because it threatens the move of a3  so they can't take so if they start  pushing up we are going to win this race  and so after we play  a1 promote to a queen  they play f7 we play queen f1 and we are  going to take this pawn and we are going  to end up winning the game so it all  comes back to the very beginning where  where should black move they should play  king  b2  this is the last version we're going to  look at it's white's move and see if you  can pause and figure out white's best  move  now the best move for white to draw this  game is to play king g6 but first let's  look at why that is the case by looking  at some of the other alternatives now if  you look at king f6  it may look like this king is  actually in between both of the pawn  structures and so this is a better move  unfortunately though  the king needs to have multiple threats  and this king is actually getting away  from this pawn on h4  to protect this pawn it really needs to  stay on the g-file this is almost  committing to  this pawn structure or stopping these  pawns on the queen side of the board so  from here black could play pawn c4  we take they take now we can start to  bring our king involved into the action  but it's too late they play c3 we take  their pawn and after a4 we're not able  to actually  make up any ground and we can't stop  their pawn from pushing forward and  promoting to the queen on  on a1  so that's not going to work just playing  king 2 f6 it's actually  doing too much harm because it's  committing to the queen side of the  board  now instead what if we just played  h5  okay well now they take on  h5 we're still going to play that king  to f6  and we've already looked it before we're  not able to catch the pawn structure  over here now why is king to g6 any  different  well the reason is because we have  multiple threats here  what if now they take on  h4  well now we can play king to f5 a square  that our king has not been at so far  before  now if they continue with their c4 we  take they take now we play king e4  remember before is on king e5 and so now  if they play c3 we capture and they play  a4 we can easily make it down and stop  the pawn coming to a one  now if we come back after we play g6  what if they don't capture with their  king what if they decide to go ahead and  commit to the queen's header board they  play c4  well now we have the move h5 and this is  the reason that g6 is so much better  because  it stops not only the threat on the  queen side but it also supports  this h pawn from pushing up the board so  now they may recognize the king can't do  anything to this pawn this pawn is  passed and it is moving up the board you  can't stop it so now if they try to  commit to the queen side of the board  we can capture  capture but we have the pawn on h6 and  we are going to beat them  to promoting we are going to  to be okay  and this is going to be a drawing game  remember  in this initial position black should be  winning if you just look at the board  state the king is about to take the pawn  on h4  and they have three pawns to double  pawns on the b file  it's very difficult if you just look at  this at face value to think that white  can actually bring out a draw remember  all we're trying to do is get a draw  from white's standpoint so as long as we  play king to g6  doesn't matter where black plays  we're going to draw this game but if we  don't play keaney g6 if we play king to  f6 or anything else  black playing correctly is going to have  an easy victory so hopefully this ready  in-game study helps you as you approach  